<header><p class="enc-kicker">Religion / idea · deity</p><h1 class="enc-title">Minerva</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Minerva was the Roman goddess of wisdom, crafts and strategic war, identified with the Greek <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/athena">Athena</a>. With Jupiter and Juno she formed the Capitoline Triad worshipped on the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/capitoline-hill">Capitol</a> in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rome">Rome</a>. Her cult spread across the empire and was often merged with local deities, as in the temple of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sulis-minerva">Sulis Minerva</a> at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bath">Bath</a>.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/minerva.jpg" alt="Minerva" width="320" height="478" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: AlMare · CC BY-SA 3.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Herculaneum_Collegio_degli_Augustali_Ercole_sull%27Olimpo_(cropped).jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Origins</h2><p>Minerva is the Roman goddess of wisdom, justice, law, victory and the sponsor of arts, trade and strategy. Her Etruscan form is Menrva, and it is disputed whether the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/roman-empire">Romans</a> borrowed her from the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/etruscans">Etruscans</a> or the reverse. Varro attributed Sabine origins to her. She may have taken her place in the Capitoline Triad, with Jupiter and Juno, under Etruscan influence, since the Etruscans had a similar group of Tinia, Uni and Menrva. From the second century BCE the Romans equated her with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/athena">Athena</a>.</p><p>Excavations at a sanctuary in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lavinium">Lavinium</a> found terracotta statues of Minerva from the 4th century BCE, with votive figures of girls and veiled women, swaddled infants and anatomical offerings. The archaeologist Fritz Graf concludes that she guarded young women in the passage to marriage and motherhood.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">What it is</h2><p>Minerva is a virgin goddess whose domain includes medicine, wisdom, commerce, weaving and the crafts. She is also a goddess of war, but of strategy rather than the violence of Mars. She is usually shown tall and armed, with a spear, and her sacred creature is the owl. Roman myth gives her the stories of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/athena">Athena</a>. She was born fully armed from the head of Jupiter, whom Vulcan split with a hammer. She made the olive tree in her contest with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/neptune">Neptune</a> over the naming of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/athens">Athens</a>, and she turned the weaver Arachne into a spider after a contest. She is thought to have invented the flute, which she threw away because it puffed out her cheeks.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">How it was used</h2><p>Her festival, the Quinquatria, ran from 19 to 23 March and was a holiday for artisans. According to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ovid">Ovid</a> the first day was her birthday, with no blood shed, and the next four days had games with drawn swords. A smaller festival of the flute-players was held on the Ides of June. According to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/livy">Livy</a> the annual nail marking the year was driven into her temple because she was thought to have invented numbers.</p><p>In Roman Britain carpenters owned tools decorated with her image for protection. At <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bath">Bath</a> she was equated with the Celtic goddess Sulis, and the two names were used together or interchangeably for the deity of the hot springs, whose healing was thought to cure ailments such as rheumatism.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Minerva” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>The Romans identified their gods with the Greek ones, Jupiter with Zeus and <b>Minerva</b> with Athena, and adopted Greek myths, art and temple forms, while the great sanctuaries of Greece flourished under Roman rule; the emperor Hadrian was initiated at Eleusis.</blockquote><a href="/religion/ancient-greek-religion">Ancient Greek religion · Spread</a></li><li><blockquote>…the earliest and fullest account of the druids and named the Gaulish gods by Roman equivalents: Mercury, he said, was the most widely venerated, followed by Apollo, <b>Minerva</b>, Mars and Jupiter, and the Gauls believed they descended from a god of the dead he likened to Dīs Pater.</blockquote><a href="/religion/celtic-religion">Celtic religion · Texts &amp; Teachers</a></li><li><blockquote>The Etruscan king Tarquinius Priscus founded a Capitoline temple to Jupiter, Juno and <b>Minerva</b>, the model for the highest official cult of the Roman world.</blockquote><a href="/religion/roman-religion">Roman religion · Origins</a></li><li><blockquote>It was composed some time before 1862 by Wallace Willis and his daughter <b>Minerva</b> Willis, who were enslaved in the old Indian Territory by a Choctaw owner.</blockquote><a href="/songs/steal-away">Steal Away</a></li><li><blockquote>&quot;Swing Low, Sweet Chariot&quot; was probably composed sometime after 1865 by Wallace Willis and his daughter <b>Minerva</b> Willis, Choctaw Freedmen living in the old Indian Territory, near Hugo in what is now Choctaw County, Oklahoma.</blockquote><a href="/songs/swing-low-sweet-chariot">Swing Low, Sweet Chariot</a></li><li><blockquote>Meeting in the Roman basilica of Santa Maria sopra <b>Minerva</b>, the conclave elected Cardinal Tommaso Parentucelli to succeed Pope Eugene IV.</blockquote><a href="/today-in-history/march-6">6 March in history</a></li></ul></section>
